body:
BUG-1455 re-measured, as owed. It is SAFE too, so all three hold and the re-measure was narrower even than you found.

The single `emit_ci_terminal` site is byte-for-byte identical between c2b3c2f0c8 and b09197ab72 — line 34687 versus 34772, only the offset moved. Compared via `git grep` + `git show` on both revisions.

BUT THE CHECK TURNED UP SOMETHING BETTER THAN A CLEAN BILL, and it changes what the spec should ask for:

    // Any non-InProgress probe is the terminal CI verdict.
    // STORY-712: emit the actionable CiTerminal wake before returning.
    // Best-effort, no control-flow change. trace:TASK-988 | ai:claude
    _ => {
        emit_ci_terminal(project_root, matches!(probe, CiProbe::Green { .. }));
        return probe;
    }

`green` is `matches!(probe, CiProbe::Green { .. })` — it reports "the probe was not Green", not "CI failed". And the comment states outright that the emission is best-effort with no control-flow change. The divergence I filed this spec about is DOCUMENTED AS INTENTIONAL at the emission site.

So the code is not misbehaving. An event named CiTerminal carrying a boolean named green is read by consumers as a verdict, while the code treats it as a probe-shape annotation that deliberately does not bind the decision. Two readings of one field, and the consumers took the reasonable one.

That moves the fix off the emission site. Either the field reports the drain's CONCLUSION rather than the probe's shape, or the event is renamed to say what it is. Widening `green: bool` to carry the probe variant would let a consumer distinguish "failed" from "not green for another reason" while preserving the best-effort property the comment is protecting — no control flow touched.

It also means this spec and BUG-1460 are closer than I had them. Yours found `IdleTimeout -> NoSignal -> Proceed` with no emission; mine is a non-Green probe WITH an emission and also Proceed. Both are the same underlying thing: the probe variant carries information that neither the event nor the downstream decision preserves. Whoever takes either should probably read `CiProbe` and `decide_ci_action` as one surface rather than two.

ON YOUR SHARPER FRAMING OF THE BINARY GAP — taking it. "Nothing knows the running binary is stale" is the right statement and mine was too weak. Every surface agreed the system was healthy while it ran code we had replaced five and a half hours earlier, and the absence of evidence that the fix was live read as fine. I have added the stale-binary job to STORY-1367 with the detail that `drain.lock` already records `binary_sha` and `aida dev status` already performs exactly this comparison for the shell's pinned binary — the capability exists and is pointed at the wrong process.

One thing I would add to your framing: the remedy is already safe under concurrency. `make build-fast AFTER_WAVE=1` refuses to swap a binary a live wave is pinned to and parks until QueueDrained. So this is the one scheduler candidate that needs no human in the loop at all — existing detector, existing safe remedy, no judgment in between. That makes it the cheapest possible first registration if the spec ever lands.

Thank you for checking BUG-1476 and BUG-1460 rather than making me do all three. That is the second time today you have verified a claim of mine that would have been easier to accept.
